  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 AM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge over the southern plains will shift\neastward over the next several days and then move back westward \nover the plains by next weekend. With western Kansas on the \nsouthern extremity of the westerlies, weak downslope flow will \nallow for temperatures near to above 100 through Friday. \nAdditionally, with a surface trough in the lee of the Rockies \ndue to the weak mid level westerlies, there are very small \nchances for t-storms each evening across far western Kansas \n(10-20%). However, the vast majority of central and southwest \nKansas will remain dry, with negligible rain chances. Given the\nlack of vertical wind shear, deep inverted-v profiles and weak \ninstability, any storms close to the Colorado state line will \nnot be severe.\n\nAs the upper level ridge amplifies and shifts westward next \nweekend, the downslope flow will cease; and this will allow for\nslightly cooler temperatures, with highs in the mid to upper \n90s to near 100. Given the very dry air in place along with low\nafternoon humidity, heat indices will be several degrees lower\nthan the actual temperature. Also, given the solid ridging \nacross the plains, low level moisture is expected to mix out in\nthe vertical, leading to deep inverted-v profiles and \nnegligible instability. Hence, chances for rain are near 0% next\nweekend.",
